## Why This Is Asked

This question tests adaptive sorting algorithms, merge logic, and the ability to reason about time complexity in practical, maintainable ways.

## Key Concepts

- **Adaptive sorting**: Using a pre-check to achieve O(n) best-case performance when the array is already sorted
- **Iterative bottom-up mergesort**: Avoiding recursion by iteratively merging sorted subarrays of increasing size
- **Stability and memory usage**: Understanding the trade-offs of O(n) auxiliary space for guaranteed stability

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortWithEarlyMerge(arr) {
  // Early return if already sorted
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) break;
    if (i === arr.length - 1) return arr;
  }

  // Iterative bottom-up mergesort
  const aux = [...arr];
  let width = 1;

  while (width < arr.length) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += width * 2) {
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, arr.length);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, arr.length);

      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right) {
        aux[k++] = arr[p] <= arr[q] ? arr[p++] : arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
    }

    [arr, aux] = [aux, arr];
    width *= 2;
  }

  return arr;
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis

- **Best case (O(n))**: Array is already sorted; single linear scan confirms this
- **Worst case (O(n log n))**: Array is reverse sorted; full mergesort required
- **Space complexity**: O(n) for the auxiliary buffer

## Why This Is Asked
This probes understanding of iterative approaches, partition logic, and complexity trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort. 

## Key Concepts
- Iterative quicksort with explicit stack
- Hoare partition scheme and pivot strategy
- Time/space complexity comparisons with mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
function iterativeQuicksort(a){
  const n=a.length;
  const stack=[[0,n-1]];
  const swap=(i,j)=>{let t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  while(stack.length){
    const [l,r]=stack.pop();
    if(l>=r) continue;
    let pivot=a[(l+r)>>1];
    let i=l, j=r;
    while(i<=j){
      while(a[i]<pivot) i++;
      while(a[j]>pivot) j--;
      if(i<=j){ swap(i,j); i++; j--; }
    }
    if(l<j) stack.push([l,j]);
    if(i<r) stack.push([i,r]);
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to guarantee O(n log n) worst-case?
- Compare stability with mergesort; when would you choose one over the other?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of two classic sorts, practical optimization, and memory trade-offs. A hybrid approach reveals ability to tune algorithms for real data patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting threshold and when it helps
- Stability via merge vs insertion behavior
- Space usage with a single auxiliary buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
function sortBatch(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const buf = new Array(n);
  function insertionSort(a, left, right) {
    for (let i = left + 1; i <= right; i++) {
      const key = a[i];
      let j = i - 1;
      while (j >= left && a[j] > key) {
        a[j + 1] = a[j];
        j--;
      }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function merge(left, mid, right) {
    let i = left, j = mid + 1, k = left;
    while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
      if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
      else buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    }
    while (i <= mid) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
    while (j <= right) buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    for (let t = left; t <= right; t++) arr[t] = buf[t];
  }
  function sort(l, r) {
    if (l >= r) return;
    if (r - l + 1 <= 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, l, r);
    } else {
      const m = Math.floor((l + r) / 2);
      sort(l, m);
      sort(m + 1, r);
      merge(l, m, r);
    }
  }
  sort(0, n - 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

